title: A 7-segment display
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
The digits on a clock radio are made of seven segments, named `a` to `g`. `seg` has one bit per segment, `seg(6)` is `g` down to `seg(0)` is `a`, and a 1 lights it:

```
 aaa
f   b
 ggg
e   c
 ddd
```

So **1** lights `b` and `c`: `"0000110"`. **0** lights everything but `g`: `"0111111"`. Those two are in the starter. Work out the others the same way.
---- task
Make `seg` show the digit `n`, for 0 to 9. Anything else shows nothing (`"0000000"`).
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity seg7 is
  port(
    n   : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    seg : out std_logic_vector(6 downto 0)
  );
end seg7;

architecture rtl of seg7 is
begin
    with n select seg <=
        "0111111" when "0000",
        "0000110" when "0001",
        -- 2 to 9 here
        "0000000" when others;
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity seg7 is
  port(
    n   : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    seg : out std_logic_vector(6 downto 0)
  );
end seg7;

architecture rtl of seg7 is
begin
    with n select seg <=
        "0111111" when "0000",
        "0000110" when "0001",
        "1011011" when "0010",
        "1001111" when "0011",
        "1100110" when "0100",
        "1101101" when "0101",
        "1111101" when "0110",
        "0000111" when "0111",
        "1111111" when "1000",
        "1101111" when "1001",
        "0000000" when others;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Draw each digit on paper and list its segments; then write them as `gfedcba`, a 1 for each lit one. 2 is `a b d e g`.
---- hint2
2 is `"1011011"`, 3 is `"1001111"`, 4 is `"1100110"`, 5 is `"1101101"`, 6 is `"1111101"`, 7 is `"0000111"`, 8 is `"1111111"`, 9 is `"1101111"`.
---- solution
```
with n select seg <=
    "0111111" when "0000",
    "0000110" when "0001",
    "1011011" when "0010",
    "1001111" when "0011",
    "1100110" when "0100",
    "1101101" when "0101",
    "1111101" when "0110",
    "0000111" when "0111",
    "1111111" when "1000",
    "1101111" when "1001",
    "0000000" when others;
```
---- done
The FPGA boards you will use have 7-segment displays, and this is the table that drives them. Next: a tiny calculator.
